WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
Right-sizing means matching the resource to the workload instead of starting from the largest familiar option.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:16.400
GPU type, GPU count, accelerator memory, CPU, system memory, local storage, remote storage throughput, and network capacity all affect whether a job runs efficiently.

3
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.400
Memory fit is often as important as raw accelerator performance.

4
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:28.000
If the model, batch, or dataset pipeline does not fit, the job may fail or require a different configuration.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:38.400
If the GPU is powerful but waits on CPU preprocessing, slow storage, or network transfer, the expensive part of the system is not doing useful work.

6
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.600
Utilization metrics help teams see what is happening.

7
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:53.600
GPU utilization, memory usage, queue wait time, job duration, failure rate, and data loading behavior can show whether the workload is compute-bound, memory-bound, input-bound, or simply oversized.

8
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.600
For early experiments, smaller or fewer GPUs may be enough.

9
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:05.200
A team can test code paths, validate data, confirm configuration, and estimate scaling behavior before moving to larger capacity.

10
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.200
That pattern reduces failed large jobs and makes learning cheaper.

11
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.400
Right-sizing is not a one-time choice.

12
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.200
Models, datasets, batch sizes, traffic patterns, runtime libraries, and business needs change.

13
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.200
A resource that was a good match last month may be wasteful or insufficient later.

14
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.400
Regular review keeps the allocation connected to reality.

15
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.800
A useful right-sizing practice records the reason for the choice.

16
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.000
If a job needs a larger GPU because of memory, write that down.

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.800
If it needs more CPU because preprocessing is heavy, record that too.

18
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.600
Future maintainers can then revisit the decision with evidence instead of guessing.
